(1) The commission shall develop, maintain, and operate a database as provided in this section.
(2) The database described in Subsection (1):
(2)(a) shall use automated data exchanges;
(2)(b) shall identify a delinquent taxpayer by identifying information;
(2)(c) may be accessed only by the commission or a depository institution;
(2)(d) shall be used to determine whether a delinquent taxpayer identified in the database has the same identifying information as that of an account holder at a depository institution; and
(2)(e) shall be updated by the commission on at least a quarterly basis.

A depository institution that determines under Section 59-1-1705 that a delinquent taxpayer identified in the depository institution data match system has the same identifying information as that of an account holder at the depository institution shall provide the commission, within five days after the day on which the depository institution accesses the depository institution data match system:
(1) the name of the account holder;
(2) the address of the account holder;
(3) the account number of the account holder;
(4) the account balance of the account holder on the day that the depository institution provides the commission the information required by this section;
(5) the type of account of the account holder;
(6) the social security number of the account holder;
(7) other information that identifies the account holder; and
(8) the name of, and contact information for, other account holders that have access to the account.

The commission shall make rules:
(1) in accordance with Title 63G, Chapter 3, Utah Administrative Rulemaking Act;
(2) defining classes of items considered to be personal property for purposes of this chapter;
(3) defining items that fall into the classes established under Subsection (2); and
(4) defining any class or item as personal property if the commission defined that class or item as personal property prior to January 1, 2004, by:
(4)(a) a rule made in accordance with Title 63G, Chapter 3, Utah Administrative Rulemaking Act;
(4)(b) a published decision of the commission; or
(4)(c) an official schedule published by the commission.

(1) Each year the commission shall prepare a record of assessment of railroads and rail car companies. The record shall include:
(1)(a) the name of the person to whom the property was assessed;
(1)(b) the number of miles in the state;
(1)(c) the number of miles in each county;
(1)(d) the total assessment of that property; and
(1)(e) the amount of the apportionment of the total assessment to each county.
(2) At least quarterly, the commission shall prepare a record of assessment of state-assessed commercial vehicles.
(3) The record of the assessment and the information upon which the assessments and apportionments are made are available for review upon request by a county assessor.

(1) Each year the commission shall prepare a record of assessment of mines. The record shall include the following information for all mines subject to assessment by the commission:
(1)(a) the owner of the mine;
(1)(b) the name and description and location of the mine;
(1)(c) the county in which the mine is located;
(1)(d) the value of the mine;
(1)(e) the value of the machinery;
(1)(f) the value of supplies and other personal property;
(1)(g) the value of improvements; and
(1)(h) the value of machinery, property, and surface improvements having a value separate and independent of the mines or mining claims assessed by the commission, and the names of the owners of the machinery, property, or surface improvements, together with any other information determined by the commission.
(2) The record of the assessment and the information upon which the assessments and apportionments are calculated are available for review upon request by a county assessor.

(1) As used in this section:
(1)(a) "Association" means the same as that term is defined in Section 57-8a-102.
(1)(b) "Common area" means:
(1)(b)(i) for a condominium project subject to Title 57, Chapter 8, Condominium Ownership Act, the following property, unless otherwise provided in the declaration or lawful amendments to the declaration:
(1)(b)(i)(A) the land included within the condominium project, whether leasehold or in fee simple;
(1)(b)(i)(B) the foundations, columns, girders, beams, supports, main walls, roofs, halls, corridors, lobbies, stairs, stairways, fire escapes, entrances, and exits of the building;
(1)(b)(i)(C) the basements, yards, gardens, parking areas, and storage spaces;
(1)(b)(i)(D) the premises for lodging of janitors or persons in charge of the property;
(1)(b)(i)(E) installations of central services such as power, light, gas, hot and cold water, heating, refrigeration, air conditioning, and incinerating;
(1)(b)(i)(F) the elevators, tanks, pumps, motors, fans, compressors, ducts, and in general all apparatus and installations existing for common use;
(1)(b)(i)(G) community and commercial facilities as may be provided for in the declaration; and
(1)(b)(i)(H) all other parts of the property necessary or convenient to the property's existence, maintenance, and safety or normally in common use; and
(1)(b)(ii) for an association subject to Title 57, Chapter 8a, Community Association Act, property that the association:
(1)(b)(ii)(A) owns;
(1)(b)(ii)(B) maintains;
(1)(b)(ii)(C) repairs; or
(1)(b)(ii)(D) administers.
(1)(c) "Condominium project" means the same as that term is defined in Section 57-8-3.
(1)(d) "Declaration" means the same as that term is defined in Section 57-8-3.
(2) In assessing the fair market value of property subject to a conservation easement under Title 57, Chapter 18, Land Conservation Easement Act, a county assessor shall consider factors relating to the property and neighboring property that affect the fair market value of the property being assessed, including:
(2)(a) value that transfers to neighboring property because of the presence of a conservation easement on the property being assessed;
(2)(b) practical and legal restrictions on the development potential of the property because of the presence of the conservation easement;
(2)(c) the absence of neighboring property similarly subject to a conservation easement to provide a basis for comparing values between properties; and
(2)(d) any other factor that causes the fair market value of the property to be affected because of the presence of a conservation easement.
(3)
(3)(a) In assessing the fair market value of a golf course or hunting club, a county assessor shall consider factors relating to the golf course or hunting club and neighboring property that affect the fair market value of the golf course or hunting club, including:
(3)(a)(i) value that transfers to neighboring property because of the presence of the golf course or hunting club;
(3)(a)(ii) practical and legal restrictions on the development potential of the golf course or hunting club; and
(3)(a)(iii) the history of operation of the golf course or hunting club and the likelihood that the present use will continue into the future.
